WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:09.830
HAASS: Well, good evening. Welcome to the
Council. I'm Richard Haass. And this is the

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:17.850
second installment in our new "What To Do
About" series. And tonight we're going to

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:23.020
be talking about what to do about Asia or,
more specifically, East Asia and the Pacific,

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:27.600
the part of the world that I believe more
than any other is likely to determine the

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.850
character of this century.

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:35.600
And there's lots of things we could focus
on tonight, but we are going to focus mainly

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:45.190
on the dynamics between China and Japan, in
many ways, the two most capable and important

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:55.710
local states, long-time not just neighbors,
but rivals. Tension has obviously been going

00:00:55.710 --> 00:01:02.780
up, given the sovereignty dispute in the East
China Sea. Military capabilities are going

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:09.480
up. Military presence is going up. And nationalism
is going up.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:16.540
You know, this competition or relationship
does not take place in a vacuum. There's lots

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:22.780
of other issues in the vicinity, including
Dennis Rodman's favorite subject, which is

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:28.870
North Korea, given other problems, say, in
the South China Sea, but tonight, we are largely

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:32.229
going to focus on them.

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:37.300
Now, what we're trying to do in this series
is really look at two issues. One is, what

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:43.150
can and should be done to prevent a crisis?
And then, secondly, if for whatever reason

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:50.311
or reasons prevention does not succeed, what
is then done to help manage a crisis? And

00:01:50.311 --> 00:01:53.920
I guess we could be creative and say, if a
crisis were to happen, what could also be

00:01:53.920 --> 00:02:01.240
done to take advantage of the crisis, not
just -- not just manage it?

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.540
And the way we're going to organize it is
essentially have a version of what you might

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:09.649
call a National Security Council meeting,
but rather than representing this or that

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:16.980
department, all three of these gentlemen I
will ask to simply be smart. And they are

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:23.340
particularly well-suited to be that, essentially,
to be ministers without portfolio or counselors,

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:29.340
but essentially wise men sitting around the
table offering their advice, so not representing

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:35.720
State or Defense or the intelligence community
or what have you, but simply being wise, experienced

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.769
hands, which -- it's exactly what they are.

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:45.640
Mike Green, who's sitting at the far end,
is at CSIS, where he's their senior vice president

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:54.010
for this part of the world. Also, he divides
his time with Georgetown University and in

00:02:54.010 --> 00:03:01.349
days past was the senior director on the National
Security Council staff for this part, again,

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:04.310
of the world.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:11.550
To his right, Jeff Bader, who has a similar
portfolio at the Brookings Institution, the

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:18.030
John Whitehead senior fellow there. And Jeff
was recently the senior director for East

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:22.569
Asian affairs on the NSC.

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:30.730
And last but least, Karl Eikenberry. Karl's
out at Stanford, so he's closer to Asia than

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:36.409
the other two these days. He was most recently
well-known as our ambassador to Afghanistan,

00:03:36.409 --> 00:03:42.000
but what's particularly relevant for the purposes
of tonight's conversation, Karl was our defense

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.959
attache in Beijing and was the senior, what,
strategic adviser at PACOM?

00:03:48.959 --> 00:03:50.299
EIKENBERRY: Strategic plans officer.

00:03:50.299 --> 00:03:57.159
HAASS: Strategic plans officer at PACOM. So
what you have here is an extraordinary combination

00:03:57.159 --> 00:04:03.620
of government experience both on the civilian
and military side and people now who are firmly

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:12.040
planted in the worlds of think-tanks and academia.
And, again, the question is, what to do about

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.799
Asia?

00:04:14.799 --> 00:04:20.161
So let's start with the first part of it.
We'll talk for a bit. And then we'll open

00:04:20.161 --> 00:04:25.450
it up and we'll get you out in plenty of time
to prepare for the State of the Union, which

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:28.260
I'm sure will be largely about this set of
questions.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.260
(LAUGHTER)

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:33.090
So this will put you in a very good position
to impress your family and your neighbors

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:39.150
later this evening.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:45.919
So let's just start with the question of prevention,
which is, how serious a challenge is this?

00:04:45.919 --> 00:04:52.000
I mean, you wake up in the morning. There's
any array of domestic and international questions.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:59.520
I was just recently at Davos. You had the
Japanese prime minister make reference to

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.580
events of 100 years ago. This is the 100th
anniversary of the outbreak of World War I,

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:09.990
and many people are drawing parallels between
the situation that existed in Europe prior

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:13.930
to the outbreak of World War I, great power
jockeying, a little bit of carelessness or

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:18.979
recklessness and so forth, rising nationalism.
We all know what that led to. Now we have

00:05:18.979 --> 00:05:24.850
the situation in the Asia Pacific. How worried
should we be?

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.030
Why don't we start -- Karl, we'll just go
across.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:33.970
EIKENBERRY: If we look back over the post-World
War II history of the Asia Pacific region,

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:42.460
it's an extraordinary success story. The economic
development that's occurred there, some 60

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:48.600
percent of the world's GDP now in the broader
Asia region. If you look at political success,

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:57.389
great success stories and democracy like Japan,
Korea, Taiwan, in through ASEAN, extraordinary

00:05:57.389 --> 00:06:06.289
success there, as well. And the fact is that
today this is an area which is largely absent

00:06:06.289 --> 00:06:08.430
hot conflict.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:13.090
But yet to your question, though, Richard,
about how much should we be concerned, yes,

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:16.970
I think we should be very concerned, because
there are some trend lines that are not in

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:21.831
the right direction at present. For example,
last year marked the first year that in the

00:06:21.831 --> 00:06:26.990
Asia Pacific region the combined defense budgets
are greater than Europe. That's the first

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:29.440
time in modern history that's occurred.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:35.910
There's a set of territorial disputes that
exist in the Asia Pacific region that, if

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:42.700
not handled properly, could lead to military
conflict. There's the problem of on the Korean

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:48.600
peninsula. So we could go on from there, and
perhaps during the course of the discussion,

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.050
I know we'll want to talk about those specifics.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:52.050
HAASS: Sure.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:55.700
BADER: Well, I'll try to say something different
from Karl, though I agree with what Karl said.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:59.580
I mean, basically, you know, we've all been
doing our speeches and articles on the Asia

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:05.820
Pacific since the Vietnam War, and they've
all been about this area as the success story

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:11.229
of the world. And I think if you were writing
-- if you were writing a national security

00:07:11.229 --> 00:07:18.990
strategy right now on the region, you would
-- you would not be repeating that narrative.

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:23.470
Instead, you'd be talking about an area with
some storm clouds. You'd be talking about

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:30.300
an area with -- with some turmoil and some
conflicts or potential conflicts.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:34.740
I mean, the Japan-China situation, if you
asked any of us 10 years ago, would we be

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:41.470
in this situation? None of us -- none of us
would have saw this. Thailand is up in smoke,

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:48.620
with seemingly no path to stability. The Japan-Korea
relationship is not going to be -- it's going

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:55.250
to be kinetic, but it's very, very bad. And
the China-Taiwan relationship is in decent

00:07:55.250 --> 00:08:00.169
shape at the moment, but you have elections
in Taiwan in 2015, and who knows where the

00:08:00.169 --> 00:08:04.949
relationship goes after that? And behind this
all, of course, you have a rising China with

00:08:04.949 --> 00:08:12.129
a very -- how should we say -- forceful and
dynamic new leader, with a forceful and dynamic

00:08:12.129 --> 00:08:13.169
foreign policy.

00:08:13.169 --> 00:08:16.470
Just the last thing I'd say in the opening
here, Richard, is -- I mean, I talk to a lot

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:24.080
of Chinese and Japanese senior officials coming
through, and I don't know anyone who is itching

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:30.280
for a fight on either side. No one. That really
is not the question. The question is, what

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:35.169
happens when all these planes and ships circling
around each other if someone has one or two

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:38.050
-- two or three many scotches one evening?

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:39.050
HAASS: Michael?

00:08:39.050 --> 00:08:45.620
GREEN: Well, on the proximate issue that you
introduced first, Richard, the Japan-China

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:51.339
problem, I would -- I would argue that the
possibility of a clash, of shots being fired

00:08:51.339 --> 00:08:56.390
is significantly higher this year than it
was the year before, and was higher the last

00:08:56.390 --> 00:09:03.230
year than it was the year before that, if
only because the amount of stuff in the East

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:07.700
China Sea and the rules of engagement are
somewhat dangerous.

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:12.970
The Japanese reported that they have scrambled
fighters to respond to Chinese fighters 80

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:18.000
percent more than they did the previous year.
The operational tempo for the white-hulled

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:23.920
coast guard ships is way up, and the gray-hulled
ships, although they're keeping a careful

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:29.720
distance, is way up. Polls in Japan and China
both show that 90 percent of the public doesn't

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:32.430
like each other.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:35.610
Stepping back one level, does that mean that
there's going to be a war? I'd say extremely

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:39.640
low probability, even if there is an accident
or something like that, for all the reasons

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:44.620
Jeff said and because of our own power and
presence.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:51.450
Stepping back one level further, do these
cracks and problems we see potentially represent

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:55.000
the beginning of disorder in Asia over the
longer term? Maybe. So we need to pay attention

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.270
to it to prevent the short-term crisis and
to make sure we're shaping the region for

00:09:58.270 --> 00:09:59.649
a more stable future in the long run.

00:09:59.649 --> 00:10:03.310
HAASS: Let me just push you on one thing,
and then we'll -- I'll turn to some just specific

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:08.160
policy questions. When you talk about it could
be the precursor of some disorder in Asia,

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:10.050
what does disorder in Asia look like?

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:17.980
GREEN: You have, as Jeff mentioned, the problems
of democracy in Thailand. You have states

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:22.010
in jeopardy. You have all the problems of
-- that other regions. But in the middle of

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:27.579
it, you have a rising China that, for the
most part, is not a revisionist power, that

00:10:27.579 --> 00:10:32.490
wants to benefit from the international system,
despite frustrations with not having had a

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:39.360
role in creating it, but not trying to overturn
the prevailing order, which is an American-led

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:40.360
order.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:44.730
If you -- what disorder looks like is that
the coercive element in Chinese diplomacy

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:50.850
today, arms -- excuse me, mercantile embargoes,
export embargoes to pressure countries on

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:57.649
territorial issues, course of use of ships
and planes, and diplomacy, it's one piece

00:10:57.649 --> 00:11:01.850
of what China does, and one piece of a much
larger story, which has some good elements

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:02.850
and bad elements.

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:07.080
That becomes the dominant narrative for the
rest of the region, because of receding U.S.

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:11.149
power. That's a very dangerous situation.
That's what disorder looks like.

00:11:11.149 --> 00:11:17.420
HAASS: OK, so let me ask, then, the basic
question. We want to influence Japan, so we've

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:23.410
got (inaudible) we've got an alliance relationship
there. So if the economists would call it

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:29.810
moral hazard, and it's the diplomatic counterpart
of it, how is it we're supportive of Japan,

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:34.110
so they don't feel they've got to do things
on their own, whether it's militarily or nuclear

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:38.350
or what have you, but without giving them
a blank check? How do we basically signal

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:45.340
American support that's -- that's considerable,
but not unconditional? Well, what is it we

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:46.430
say? What is it we do?

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:51.389
EIKENBERRY: Well, I think -- going back to
what Mike had said about making sure that,

00:11:51.389 --> 00:11:57.810
first and foremost, our foundation for the
entire Asia Pacific region is engagement and

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:03.339
strength, and part of that is retooling our
economy and ensuring that we remain economically

00:12:03.339 --> 00:12:07.820
relevant in the region, and that probably
is one of the biggest threats that the United

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:10.260
States is facing now.

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:17.910
Secondly, in terms then of our military posture
in the Asia Pacific region, to ensure that

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:25.370
we maintain the capabilities for base, that
we maintain strong alliances and partners

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:29.910
throughout the region, that also then gives
a context for the relationship with Japan.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:34.070
HAASS: Well, let me just push you on that.
Does that entail increasing, for example,

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:38.120
the quality or quantity of our air and naval
presence?

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:45.990
EIKENBERRY: It does, yes. Japan's concerns
right now -- they have one concern with -- as

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:50.601
we all do -- with the Korean peninsula and
with North Korean threat, but I think their

00:12:50.601 --> 00:12:56.680
deeper long-term concern is the rise of China
and the rise of China's military capabilities

00:12:56.680 --> 00:13:01.860
behind that. And that's a concern that's reflected
throughout the Asia Pacific region.

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:09.130
And if we want to shape in a positive way
the development of China and to try to encourage

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:16.589
its further integration into regional architectures
and global architectures, then you do need

00:13:16.589 --> 00:13:22.480
a position of strength to do that. And with
that strength then comes reassurance to our

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:23.480
allies.

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:28.000
HAASS: Let me ask a question, then, slightly
different, Jeff, which is, OK, if we should

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:31.910
maybe increase our military presence, which
largely means air and naval -- let's put aside

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:36.580
the question of Marines at Darwin. I never
quite understood how 2,000 Marines in the

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:41.670
Asia Pacific was going to tip the military
balance, but I'm sure that's my shortcoming.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:48.850
The -- what about diplomatically doing more?
I mean, should we essentially -- we, the United

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:55.710
States -- insert ourselves in any way, I mean,
in this issue between Japan and China? Should

00:13:55.710 --> 00:14:01.389
we have an American diplomatic initiative
about the legal political disposition of the

00:14:01.389 --> 00:14:07.440
disputed islands? Or should we simply stand
back and let these guys try to sort it out?

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:13.600
I mean, is this a place where we ought to,
if you will, rebalance some of our diplomatic

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:15.380
activity towards this part of the world?

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:20.180
BADER: A few thoughts, Richard. And when I
was in the administration, we spent a lot

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:25.821
of effort on rebalancing our diplomatic presence
in Asia, particularly in Southeast Asia, but

00:14:25.821 --> 00:14:29.570
I think we're not in Southeast Asia here now,
so I won't get into that, but there was a

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:31.029
lot of effort to do that.

00:14:31.029 --> 00:14:36.461
Now, the Japan-China situation and then the
Japan-Korea situation, the administration

00:14:36.461 --> 00:14:44.110
has put a lot of effort into quiet diplomacy
between the two. Joe Biden has been on the

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:48.649
phone with all -- you know, with the various
leaders. He's been out there. John Kerry,

00:14:48.649 --> 00:14:56.880
as well. I would say the results have been
somewhere between minimal and negative, judging

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:02.100
by the trend lines. I say this not to be critical
of the individuals, but let's face it, these

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:12.040
are three (inaudible) Japan and South Korea,
who are, I would say, very friction-tolerant.

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:18.130
Each one, it's in their -- it's in the interest
of each one of them to have difficult relations

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:23.420
with the others. It's in their political domestic
interests, and that is something we cannot

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:26.070
(inaudible) dynamic, we cannot overcome that.

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:30.380
HAASS: Can I just interrupt you on one point,
just so I understand? So whatever level of

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:35.200
diplomatic activity we've been carrying has
had results that you describe between negligible,

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:42.259
I think it was, or modest, whatever it was,
and negative, would more diplomacy have a

00:15:42.259 --> 00:15:45.810
positive result? Or does more diplomacy actually
risk having a larger negative?

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:50.740
BADER: I mean, I think -- you know, I was
being slightly facetious, but not entirely.

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:56.610
We have to be engaged privately with them.
We've certainly -- between the Japanese and

00:15:56.610 --> 00:16:02.750
the Koreans, we certainly have to play a role,
since they can't seem to work it out themselves.

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:06.770
With the Japanese and the Chinese, clearly,
as you -- you know, you raised the issue of

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:11.770
moral hazard, the administration's been trying
to convey to the Japanese since the middle

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:18.520
of 2011, don't do anything reckless with the
islands. Kurt Campbell conveyed that message

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:19.540
before Noda...

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:20.540
(CROSSTALK)

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:22.699
HAASS: It's kind of what we used to tell Taiwan.

00:16:22.699 --> 00:16:23.699
(CROSSTALK)

00:16:23.699 --> 00:16:24.699
BADER: Hmm?

00:16:24.699 --> 00:16:26.640
HAASS: It was kind of what we used to often
tell Taiwan, don't do anything reckless to

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:27.650
change the status quo.

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:33.199
BADER: And then they went ahead and did it.
I mean, look, as the great power in the region,

00:16:33.199 --> 00:16:37.620
we have no choice. We have to be engaged.
The powers -- each of the powers actually

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:45.020
wants us engaged privately. None of them wants
us out there publicly taking sides, seeking

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:49.350
to embarrass, or putting forward formulas
for them to come to, like the, let's say,

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:50.350
Middle East peace...

00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:52.750
HAASS: Well, it's worked so well there. We
thought we might transfer it to this part

00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:54.230
of the world.

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:55.480
(LAUGHTER)

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:59.889
So then, what then is the nature of American
diplomatic engagement, if it's not to resolve

00:16:59.889 --> 00:17:05.730
the questions of sovereignty? You know, I've
heard everything from, you know, creating

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:13.400
special types of environmental parks and,
you know, sort of whatever to -- I mean, is

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:17.520
there a substantive initiative, do we think?
Or is it simply, don't change the status quo

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:21.049
unilaterally by force? Is that the substance
of our diplomatic message?

00:17:21.049 --> 00:17:26.910
GREEN: You're talking about the Japan-China
piece and the Senkaku-Diaoyu-type problem?

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:32.690
Look, for CFR or Brookings or CSIS, we ought
to dive into the deep end of that pool and

00:17:32.690 --> 00:17:38.450
see what we can do in terms of intellectual
facilitation. For the U.S. government, Japan's

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:43.660
an ally, and we cannot be in a position of
brokering between an ally and a power that

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:49.860
is testing not only Japan on the Senkakus
and Diaoyu ties, but all along the first island

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:50.860
chain.

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:57.419
If you're worried about China grabbing the
islands or doing something provocative, you

00:17:57.419 --> 00:18:01.360
want to hew close to Japan. If you read about
Japan doing something provocative, you want

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:06.320
to hew closely to Japan. We don't have a joint
and combined alliance with the Japanese the

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:12.240
way we do with NATO or with Korea. The Japanese
didn't want it. And for decades, it was the

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:16.710
Japanese that worried about being entrapped
in our war in Vietnam or in China and Korea.

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:19.740
And now, you know, for about the past decade,
we've started worrying a little bit about

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:21.870
maybe being entrapped in their war for the
first time.

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:28.330
The answer is to move towards more jointness,
more joint exercises, more concept of operations,

00:18:28.330 --> 00:18:31.700
so that we're inside the Japanese decision-making
loops and we're making decisions together

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:36.440
and we're dissuading China. The worst thing
we could do is to step back and sort of leave

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:44.570
it to the Japanese to sort out or to somehow
broker, which would only encourage Japanese

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:50.230
hedging, which is unhelpful, and Chinese hopes
that perhaps there's a way to drive a wedge

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:51.230
between us.

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:55.539
HAASS: Karl, you spent time in Beijing as
the attache. What message do we give the Chinese

00:18:55.539 --> 00:19:00.530
about restraint? And then, if they go, well,
hold it, does that mean under any and all

00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:08.400
circumstances, well, no way? What is it tell
the Chinese in order to, again, behave responsibly,

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:13.980
if they say, but what if the Japanese do X,
Y and Z? Do you still expect us to show restraint,

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:16.110
even if the Japanese are not?

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:19.790
EIKENBERRY: Yeah, this is a -- look this is
a very difficult problem with regard to the

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:26.710
Senkaku and Diaoyu. And the consequences of
getting this wrong, or as Jeff had said, a

00:19:26.710 --> 00:19:32.530
Japanese fighter pilot making a mistake and
a shooting match starting, almost certainly

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:41.470
not leading to a war, but really setting Asia
back and our own position in Asia back tremendously,

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:45.120
that it's rising with every day.

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:51.000
Last year, Mike talked about fighter sorties
from the Japanese air self-defense force over

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:56.640
the islands. Six hundred last year scrambled.
That's two a day. And it's a matter of time

00:19:56.640 --> 00:19:59.940
until -- it's just a matter of time until
something happens.

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:04.800
Now, the tough part about this for us is that
we do have an alliance with Japan, and that

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:12.630
alliance specifically covers the Senkakus
and Diaoyu. And if, according to the treaty,

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:19.080
that if the Japanese are attacked in the Senkaku-Diaoyu,
then we have a treaty obligation there.

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:24.970
Now, we don't go to war based upon lawyers'
interpretations of treaties, of course. And

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:30.280
a point I would like to make about this is
that I remember as a colonel working in the

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:36.730
office of secretary of defense when some island
occurred -- dustup occurred in the Asia Pacific

00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:38.070
region. I called an old State Department...

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:39.620
HAASS: It wasn't Kenmoya Matsu (ph), was it?

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:43.890
EIKENBERRY: It -- no, I'm a little bit -- a
little bit beyond that one.

00:20:43.890 --> 00:20:44.890
(LAUGHTER)

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:45.890
HAASS: OK. Just checking.

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:50.929
EIKENBERRY: But I called up a very senior
retired diplomat, who I thought a lot of,

00:20:50.929 --> 00:20:55.100
and asked for his opinion. He said, Karl,
remember the first rule here. The United States

00:20:55.100 --> 00:21:01.080
of America is a great power and great powers
don't go to war over rocks. Having said that,

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:06.390
though, these are not just rocks to the Japanese
and the Chinese. We know how sensitive this

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:07.890
issue is.

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:13.450
Your point about -- then your question, what
do we tell the Chinese? Well, I think that

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:19.640
the Chinese are observing our diplomacy right
now, and clearly they see how we're engaging

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:26.140
with Tokyo, as we did in the aftermath that
Prime Minister Abe's visit to the Yasukuni

00:21:26.140 --> 00:21:34.700
shrine. They are clearly getting messages
from us that we want to see Tokyo and Beijing

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:36.440
talking to each other about this.

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:44.880
Tokyo -- Beijing's position is that, as long
as Tokyo will admit there's a dispute here,

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:50.370
then we can sit down and talk, and Tokyo has
not found the way at this point to reach that

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:54.010
formula, but I'd be optimistic that they could
with some clever diplomacy.

00:21:54.010 --> 00:21:57.750
HAASS: Let me ask two more questions on the
prevention side of the coin. The Chinese recently

00:21:57.750 --> 00:22:04.070
-- must be, what, several months ago now -- announced
the air defense identification zone. Is there

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:11.261
anything we can or should do vis-a-vis that
that would basically send messages such as,

00:22:11.261 --> 00:22:19.220
we believe, to decrease the chance of a crisis
or an incident, in terms of respecting it,

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:24.221
challenging it, what have you? What -- the
Chinese put that out here. It was, if you

00:22:24.221 --> 00:22:29.830
will, a unilateral challenge to the status
quo. What should we be doing about it?

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:35.679
BADER: Well, the -- I mean, DOD responded
instantly, within 24 hours, by sending a B-52

00:22:35.679 --> 00:22:40.390
over the area, which I think was absolutely
the right -- the right signal. I mean, I think

00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:45.510
it was -- you know, there are two issues,
military aircraft and civilian aircraft, and

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:49.070
we got somewhat crosswise in our messaging
on that. The military...

00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:50.440
HAASS: Can you just explain where we came
out on that?

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:54.470
BADER: Yeah, where we came out was -- DOD
was very clear, this was not going to affect

00:22:54.470 --> 00:23:01.390
in any fashion military aircraft operations
inside the ADIZ, no notification, they would

00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:05.330
continue as before, which is absolutely the
right call. We have a lot of -- you know,

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:10.900
a lot of operations in that zone, and we're
not about to announce them to the Chinese

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:16.210
or ask permission. We have no obligation under
international law, international practices

00:23:16.210 --> 00:23:17.559
to do so.

00:23:17.559 --> 00:23:23.380
On the civilian side, I mean, both Hagel and
Kerry made clear statements that the way our

00:23:23.380 --> 00:23:29.650
ADIZ works is you only have a reporting requirement
if you are flying towards the country, towards

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:35.600
the United States. If you're just transiting
the zone, no reporting obligation whatsoever.

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:39.910
And the Chinese announcement makes no such
distinction, so a plane -- you know, an ANA

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:45.400
flight from Tokyo down to Singapore that cuts
through the zone nominally has to report or

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:51.250
the Chinese can scramble something to see
what's going on, which is not a healthy situation.

00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:59.070
I think that the FAA put out a general guidance
saying, we respect ADIZs and we expect civilian

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:04.610
aircraft to honor them, OK? That wasn't very
well coordinated within the U.S. government,

00:24:04.610 --> 00:24:07.650
frankly. It send confusing signals. The Japanese
didn't know quite what to make of it.

00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:09.080
HAASS: Should we amend it, now?

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:10.080
BADER: Should we have...

00:24:10.080 --> 00:24:12.850
HAASS: Should we now amend it?

00:24:12.850 --> 00:24:20.440
BADER: I mean, the FAA's view was, we don't
use our civilian aircraft as experiments or,

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:25.740
you know, that they're -- we don't put civilians
at risk because of diplomatic purposes. And

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:31.850
I understood what the FAA was doing. It was
the -- I'm not sure, Richard. I don't know

00:24:31.850 --> 00:24:35.870
the answer to that. It was just badly coordinated.
I don't know if I'd go beyond that.

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:40.340
EIKENBERRY: On my last United flight to Beijing,
I actually supported the FAA position.

00:24:40.340 --> 00:24:41.340
(LAUGHTER)

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:46.250
BADER: That's the point. I mean, you know,
those of us who did KOL-7 (ph) 30 years ago...

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:48.350
HAASS: Exactly. I remember that.

00:24:48.350 --> 00:24:53.770
BADER: But I think the important thing to
warn China is, don't do this again in the

00:24:53.770 --> 00:24:58.640
South China Sea or in the Yellow Sea. I think
that's the great risk, that they'll -- particularly

00:24:58.640 --> 00:25:02.720
in the South China Sea, where you've got a
whole bunch of claimants, not just one claimant,

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:05.880
and that would really be -- I think that would
really be a provocation if they would do that.

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:11.730
HAASS: I guess this is the last question,
and I'll start with Mike, which is, diplomatically,

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:17.650
in the interests of preventing a crisis or
conceivably better managing one, if it were

00:25:17.650 --> 00:25:23.710
to happen all the same, why not make a big
push for introducing a set of confidence-building

00:25:23.710 --> 00:25:28.961
measures in this part of the world, incidents
at sea, various types of communications, or

00:25:28.961 --> 00:25:35.980
even some version of the Conference on Security
and Cooperation in Europe, some type of a

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:40.590
more structured security system for this part
of the world, which have certain rules of

00:25:40.590 --> 00:25:44.230
the road, certain obligations, certain mechanisms
for stabilizing.

00:25:44.230 --> 00:25:49.460
This part of the world is drowning, literally
and figuratively, in economic arrangements

00:25:49.460 --> 00:25:56.540
and, in many ways, is extraordinarily thin
in diplomatic and in political-military arrangements.

00:25:56.540 --> 00:26:00.059
So why not now the -- why wouldn't we as a
government -- and to the extent we represent

00:26:00.059 --> 00:26:06.900
the United States government here -- why wouldn't
we take this initiative to introduce essentially

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:14.380
a set of proposals to help regulate, if you
will, or increase the structure of the Asia

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:17.160
Pacific in a political-military sense? Why
not do that?

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:23.000
GREEN: We should. There is more happening
just in the last year or two on that front

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:28.160
than those of us who watch the region have
seen in decades. The ADMM-Plus, which is a

00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:34.280
Southeast Asian-based defense cooperation
summit, is now holding regular multilateral

00:26:34.280 --> 00:26:39.169
exercises almost on a monthly basis, and that's
-- that's long overdue.

00:26:39.169 --> 00:26:45.850
The U.S. has been urging China since Karl
was a colonel in the Pentagon, working on

00:26:45.850 --> 00:26:55.090
China, to implement a piece of paper we signed
on maritime military transparency and confidence-building.

00:26:55.090 --> 00:26:57.550
And the Japanese would like something like
that, and the Chinese won't even talk to them

00:26:57.550 --> 00:26:58.550
about it.

00:26:58.550 --> 00:27:03.350
So we should be doing that. I think the PLA
-- and Karl knows this well -- have a different

00:27:03.350 --> 00:27:08.070
view of transparency and what it means. They
would like, as a matter of principle for us,

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:11.210
to stop doing surveillance, to stop doing
things we've been doing for 60 years in the

00:27:11.210 --> 00:27:15.080
region. If we can agree on some of those principles,
they'll talk to us about transparency.

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:19.520
So we'll make some progress in multilateral
exercises. We'll have frustration with confidence-building.

00:27:19.520 --> 00:27:21.720
But that should be a central part of our effort.

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:29.260
And if I could real quick, the -- the Chinese,
as Karl said, want Japan to acknowledge de

00:27:29.260 --> 00:27:34.870
jure, not just de facto, that there's a dispute.
I think the Japanese could do that, but they

00:27:34.870 --> 00:27:38.029
can't do it under coercion, and we shouldn't
want them to do it under coercion.

00:27:38.029 --> 00:27:41.470
So this is an area where the U.S. could have
a role. It's not brokering, but with clear

00:27:41.470 --> 00:27:45.390
signals and organizing our friends and allies
in the region and partners, make it clear

00:27:45.390 --> 00:27:48.160
to Beijing they're not going to get any progress
and we're going to hold them accountable,

00:27:48.160 --> 00:27:52.130
as long as they resort to coercion to try
to undermine Japan's claim to administrative

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:53.529
control of those islands.

00:27:53.529 --> 00:27:58.810
But if there's some basis to remove that coercive
tool, there might then be a basis for Japan

00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:04.350
and China to have some -- I mean, the game
would be Japan would call on China to go to

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:07.029
the international court, knowing the Chinese
wouldn't go, but they will have made -- so

00:28:07.029 --> 00:28:11.580
there is room for us to play, but it can't
be ecumenical in the sense we need to recognize

00:28:11.580 --> 00:28:15.340
that the Chinese coercion is a major problem
for all of us in this.

00:28:15.340 --> 00:28:18.780
HAASS: OK, so let's -- let's do something
the U.S. government is not terribly good at,

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:25.140
which is contingency planning. And so let's
assume that at some point either some fighter

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:30.890
pilot on one or another side will -- his testosterone
will get out of control and he will do something

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:35.830
quite aggressive, or some ship captain will
have too much to drink and he will do something

00:28:35.830 --> 00:28:42.590
quite dumb, and there's an incident, and there's
a collision of aircraft, ships, whatever.

00:28:42.590 --> 00:28:47.820
What do we do? What is it we immediately do?
Because one of the things that's not obvious

00:28:47.820 --> 00:28:54.130
to me is the locals. They don't have dedicated
communications links. They don't have a large

00:28:54.130 --> 00:28:59.520
amount of experience in managing crises. So
what is it the United States does, if you

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:04.890
will, to keep an incident just an incident,
rather than the seed of escalation?

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:08.429
EIKENBERRY: You want to start with that one?

00:29:08.429 --> 00:29:12.990
BADER: I'll start on that. We do have crisis
communication links, the U.S. and China.

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:14.059
HAASS: The U.S. -- yeah...

00:29:14.059 --> 00:29:15.059
(CROSSTALK)

00:29:15.059 --> 00:29:20.590
BADER: We have, you know, I think CNO with
his -- the chief of naval operations with

00:29:20.590 --> 00:29:28.750
his counterpart, president has a secure link
with Xi Jinping. We've used them. And presumably,

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:35.320
those would be activated right away. The Japanese
and Chinese actually do have some links, but

00:29:35.320 --> 00:29:39.500
they just haven't utilized them -- utilized
them much.

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:49.250
You know, in 2010, when a drunken Chinese
sea captain bumped a Japanese coast guard

00:29:49.250 --> 00:29:56.640
vessel, a few of us were out there immediately
talking about Article V and mutual security

00:29:56.640 --> 00:30:01.520
treaty, to try to send the clear signal to
the Chinese that this -- that this had consequences.

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:08.380
And, you know, what we've tried to communicate
to the Japanese is, the alliance is firm and

00:30:08.380 --> 00:30:14.430
seamless, but please don't make rocks the
centerpiece of the alliance.

00:30:14.430 --> 00:30:20.309
You know, what you do in a crisis, I think,
you know, this is really Karl's world, but

00:30:20.309 --> 00:30:26.240
I -- I mean, I have no doubt there would be
movements orchestrated from your coast guard

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:36.520
and from the region to have some presence.
But I think the assumption has been that the

00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:42.549
Japanese are reasonably capable of taking
care of themselves in a small-scale incident,

00:30:42.549 --> 00:30:49.350
and the U.S. doesn't have to come and shouldn't
come charging in, which would, you know, escalate...

00:30:49.350 --> 00:30:50.350
(CROSSTALK)

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:55.019
HAASS: But do we move U.S. air and naval assets?
I mean, quite honestly, if there is an incident

00:30:55.019 --> 00:31:01.320
-- and say there's some scrambling of Japanese
or Chinese aircraft, there's some movement

00:31:01.320 --> 00:31:05.370
of vessels, what do we do? And do we physically
in any way try to interpose ourselves? Do

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:08.440
we hold back? What's our role in this?

00:31:08.440 --> 00:31:15.270
EIKENBERRY: Well, in terms of Japanese capabilities,
military capabilities, should there be an

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:21.940
incident around the Senkaku-Diaoyu, then initially
the Japanese do have the capabilities to take

00:31:21.940 --> 00:31:24.870
care of themselves, so to speak.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:30.580
The United States really wouldn't have to
move forces, Richard, into the area. In fact,

00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:35.640
that could be -- depending upon how the situation
evolved, that could be very counterproductive,

00:31:35.640 --> 00:31:41.190
because the Chinese could be interpreting
this, then, as a reinforcement of the Japanese.

00:31:41.190 --> 00:31:45.920
And until we understood better what Beijing's
intents were, I think that that could be very

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:47.070
escalatory.

00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:51.190
On the other hand, we have a remarkable set
of capabilities that are over the horizon

00:31:51.190 --> 00:31:57.049
up in space that we can be very useful to
the Japanese military, in terms of helping

00:31:57.049 --> 00:31:59.000
them understand, get situational awareness.

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:05.340
I'd go a step further. If we'd -- if we were
to be sending signals, which we would immediately

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:13.159
about trying to de-escalate rapidly and trying
to get the Japanese and the Chinese to both

00:32:13.159 --> 00:32:18.941
be saying from Beijing and Tokyo that they
wanted to de-escalate this, that we could

00:32:18.941 --> 00:32:27.970
also play perhaps a role in terms of providing
transparency to all sides that would be reassuring

00:32:27.970 --> 00:32:33.919
that might help them to have the confidence
to go forward diplomatically than -- and to

00:32:33.919 --> 00:32:34.919
begin to talk.

00:32:34.919 --> 00:32:41.679
GREEN: I may be the outlier, but -- and it
depends a lot on how this happened. You know,

00:32:41.679 --> 00:32:46.269
did the Japanese provoke by putting a lighthouse
up? Did the Chinese, you know, land? I mean,

00:32:46.269 --> 00:32:51.610
a lot of it depends on how this happened.

00:32:51.610 --> 00:32:56.950
But with that as a caveat, if this was -- if
this involves -- if it looks like the Scarborough

00:32:56.950 --> 00:32:57.950
Shoal incident...

00:32:57.950 --> 00:32:58.950
HAASS: Explain what that is.

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:03.340
GREEN: So what happened in Scarborough Shoal
between the Philippines and China was, the

00:33:03.340 --> 00:33:07.559
Philippines and China both claimed the waters.
The Philippines have been fishing it for a

00:33:07.559 --> 00:33:12.789
millennium. The U.S. tried to broker in that
case an agreement for the forces to stand

00:33:12.789 --> 00:33:17.480
off. The Chinese side came in anyway. The
Philippines Navy took one of these old Coast

00:33:17.480 --> 00:33:21.490
Guard cutters we gave them and went steaming
with flags flying and drums and bugles playing

00:33:21.490 --> 00:33:25.760
right out into this horseshoe-shaped atoll,
and the Chinese cut them off. They didn't

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:28.610
have food and water, and they basically pulled
out, and they haven't operated there since.

00:33:28.610 --> 00:33:31.330
The Chinese now have effective operational
control.

00:33:31.330 --> 00:33:37.640
If that happened with this East China Sea
against Japan, the strategic implications

00:33:37.640 --> 00:33:42.549
for us across the region, in terms of how
others view our staying power, the credibility

00:33:42.549 --> 00:33:46.059
of our security commitments, and how China
views them, could be really devastating. So

00:33:46.059 --> 00:33:49.340
we'd have to take that into consideration.
It doesn't mean we move forces. Probably we

00:33:49.340 --> 00:33:52.770
don't move forces into the immediate area,
because my guess is, the Japanese will pull

00:33:52.770 --> 00:33:54.610
back.

00:33:54.610 --> 00:33:57.650
But if we're going to ask the Japanese to
stand down, we're going to have to do something.

00:33:57.650 --> 00:34:01.769
And I think, if this were a simulated NSC,
there would be very serious consideration

00:34:01.769 --> 00:34:06.510
about flexible deterrent options, as they're
called, B2s (ph), and so forth, moving to

00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:10.109
Guam. Not a lot of press necessarily, but
there's going to have to be a little bit of

00:34:10.109 --> 00:34:15.530
muscle, not in the middle of the fight, maybe
from space, maybe other assets, and declaratory

00:34:15.530 --> 00:34:21.050
policy that is just enough to let the Japanese
feel confident and the rest of the region

00:34:21.050 --> 00:34:25.099
feel confident, but not so much that we provoke
escalation. That's a tough call. That's why

00:34:25.099 --> 00:34:26.310
it depends on exactly how it unfolds.

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:31.400
HAASS: But isn't, then, the most complicated
scenario one in which the Japanese seem to

00:34:31.400 --> 00:34:36.470
be the ones who have initiated it? It's one
thing, if the Chinese do certain things, then

00:34:36.470 --> 00:34:41.240
it's almost more straightforward. But it seems
to me by far the most complicated policy for

00:34:41.240 --> 00:34:43.849
-- scenario for the United States is one in
which the Japanese, quote, unquote, "seem

00:34:43.849 --> 00:34:46.110
to be at fault."

00:34:46.110 --> 00:34:48.460
GREEN: Are you looking at me?

00:34:48.460 --> 00:34:49.460
HAASS: I'll look at...

00:34:49.460 --> 00:34:50.590
GREEN: As the Japan guy?

00:34:50.590 --> 00:34:51.590
HAASS: Yeah.

00:34:51.590 --> 00:34:55.679
GREEN: And then I started studying Korean
again. No, I think then you get to the prevention

00:34:55.679 --> 00:34:58.339
problem. That's why I mentioned earlier, we
need to be inside the planning loop. And...

00:34:58.339 --> 00:34:59.339
(CROSSTALK)

00:34:59.339 --> 00:35:01.970
HAASS: OK. This assumes -- we assume that
we missed that opportunity, for whatever reason.

00:35:01.970 --> 00:35:07.160
GREEN: Then we have a problem. But then what
we -- we start to create it quickly, and we

00:35:07.160 --> 00:35:11.840
start talking about scenario planning, contingency
planning. The other thing that I think...

00:35:11.840 --> 00:35:13.330
HAASS: That's what we're talking about now.
Mike, basically...

00:35:13.330 --> 00:35:15.430
GREEN: No, I mean -- I mean, in the crisis.
That's what you have to do.

00:35:15.430 --> 00:35:16.430
HAASS: Yeah. Yeah.

00:35:16.430 --> 00:35:22.099
GREEN: The other thing I think that's important
is to -- this is not the right choice of words,

00:35:22.099 --> 00:35:26.970
in a way, but regionalize the problem. In
other words, you could see with Prime Minister

00:35:26.970 --> 00:35:30.359
Abe that part of Japan's playbook to deal
with a rising China is to strengthen ties

00:35:30.359 --> 00:35:35.440
with Australia, India, Philippines. Korea
would be nice, but it's not happening.

00:35:35.440 --> 00:35:41.820
I think in these kinds of crises, we want
our Australian friends, other actors to weigh

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:48.730
in and to echo the messages of reassurance
and restraint. And that kind of external solidarity

00:35:48.730 --> 00:35:53.640
will help and also send a signal to Beijing
that the region has a stake in how this plays.

00:35:53.640 --> 00:35:58.270
BADER: Richard, if I could just add a word,
I think that the basic formula would be to

00:35:58.270 --> 00:36:04.580
have highly visible support for Japan combined
with extremely strong messaging behind the

00:36:04.580 --> 00:36:11.960
scenes to them, not to interpret this visible
support as anything resembling a blank check

00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:13.260
or a green light.

00:36:13.260 --> 00:36:17.890
I mean, I went through something like this
in late 2010 with North and South Korea, after

00:36:17.890 --> 00:36:23.640
the North Koreans shelled the South Korean
islands. And we had to face two questions.

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:27.500
One is, do we support the South Koreans in
a follow-up exercise? And we had a big debate

00:36:27.500 --> 00:36:32.331
about it and -- most of us, and the consensus
was, yes, we had to, and be visible, and we

00:36:32.331 --> 00:36:36.210
had a carrier in the region. But we also at
the same time stuck, you know, put Mike Mullen

00:36:36.210 --> 00:36:42.390
on a plane to go out there and tell them,
you can -- don't you do any of the things

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:45.609
that you are threatening to do that would
escalate the situation. So there was a big

00:36:45.609 --> 00:36:47.130
difference between the public and the private
messaging.

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:51.630
HAASS: Let me use the Middle East again as
an analogy, in this case where a crisis did

00:36:51.630 --> 00:36:57.820
lead to extremely productive diplomacy, which
was the '73 war. And you had certain things,

00:36:57.820 --> 00:37:02.420
and out of that, Henry Kissinger was able
to do the disengagement agreements and ultimately

00:37:02.420 --> 00:37:07.880
it set in motion what years later became also
an Egyptian-Israeli rapprochement.

00:37:07.880 --> 00:37:11.142
Is there anything about a crisis in this part
of the world that we can run with it, not

00:37:11.142 --> 00:37:15.369
that we want one to happen, but if you've
got lemons, make lemonade? Is there any lemonade

00:37:15.369 --> 00:37:16.500
to be made here?

00:37:16.500 --> 00:37:20.980
EIKENBERRY: I don't know, Richard. In the
case of the Korean peninsula, I could see

00:37:20.980 --> 00:37:29.030
a path ahead where if there was to be a -- if
there were to be an act of war, that perhaps

00:37:29.030 --> 00:37:33.589
where we are with China right now and the
Republic of Korea, there could be opportunity,

00:37:33.589 --> 00:37:38.550
but in this instance, where we're talking
about the East China Sea or the South China

00:37:38.550 --> 00:37:46.900
Sea, I don't see very clearly where the opportunity
would be. I think for now, as I see it, avoidance

00:37:46.900 --> 00:37:49.040
is critical here.

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:51.080
HAASS: Jeff?

00:37:51.080 --> 00:37:56.750
BADER: The dispute between China and Japan
is not about the Senkakus. The Senkakus is

00:37:56.750 --> 00:38:01.650
an excuse. I mean, from the Chinese perspective,
I think there are three things, number one,

00:38:01.650 --> 00:38:10.560
is a visceral, gut resentment of Japan for
its historic role vis-a-vis China in the last

00:38:10.560 --> 00:38:18.829
century. Number two is a tactical desire to
use -- to paint Japan as reverting to the

00:38:18.829 --> 00:38:23.150
1930s, because they're trying to slow down
Abe's building up of the security capabilities.

00:38:23.150 --> 00:38:28.700
So it's gut, and it's also highly calculated.
And the third piece of it is just terror on

00:38:28.700 --> 00:38:34.980
the part of the Chinese leaders of being -- of
appearing to be soft on Japan, because of

00:38:34.980 --> 00:38:37.880
the impact that that might have on their leadership.

00:38:37.880 --> 00:38:41.240
HAASS: So then why wouldn't the answer to
your -- if there were a crisis -- I agree

00:38:41.240 --> 00:38:45.140
with you. So the answer's not to necessarily
to -- I wouldn't be against it -- negotiating

00:38:45.140 --> 00:38:49.150
some new legal or whatever -- political, legal
framework for the islands, but that would

00:38:49.150 --> 00:38:53.720
be the time to basically say, this is a relationship
that needs managing. And what we really want

00:38:53.720 --> 00:38:59.290
to do is put in a much thicker web of ties,
whether it's to prevent crises or deal with

00:38:59.290 --> 00:39:03.890
crises, whether it's bilateral or regional,
but that seems to me -- if a crisis scared

00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:08.190
people about what could happen, and stock
markets around the region plummeted, there

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:12.820
is an argument -- at least that would be the
time to do some things that now might be resented,

00:39:12.820 --> 00:39:16.190
but in those conditions, there might actually
be a greater opening.

00:39:16.190 --> 00:39:22.590
GREEN: You might get that effect for a while,
clearly, if the U.S. played its role rightly.

00:39:22.590 --> 00:39:28.010
There's a loss of face. If we -- together
with Japan -- somehow force China to back

00:39:28.010 --> 00:39:33.570
down, that has very dangerous implications
for us domestically, to a lesser extent, but

00:39:33.570 --> 00:39:35.690
still also true in Japan.

00:39:35.690 --> 00:39:39.540
If we play it right, we might -- especially
if the stock markets crash and the economic

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:45.050
players come in -- we might start to get a
sort of -- a more stable situation, but it'll

00:39:45.050 --> 00:39:49.810
be like the 1995-'96 Taiwan Straits crisis,
where the militaries were so calculating where

00:39:49.810 --> 00:39:54.070
their vulnerabilities were, where our vulnerabilities
are, and how they build up their force posture

00:39:54.070 --> 00:39:56.370
over the next decade so they can win next
time.

00:39:56.370 --> 00:40:00.240
So there's almost -- I hate to sound so pessimistic
-- there's almost no way we come out of this

00:40:00.240 --> 00:40:09.490
with a happy, you know, recognition of how
dangerous things were. You know, I -- it's

00:40:09.490 --> 00:40:13.510
-- I don't see the Cuban missile crisis, you
know, scenario necessarily. There's going

00:40:13.510 --> 00:40:17.099
to be learning. Some of it will be good; some
of it will be how to hedge better and prepare

00:40:17.099 --> 00:40:18.349
better for the future.

00:40:18.349 --> 00:40:28.349
EIKENBERRY: I think also that we need to look
at Senkaku-Diaoyu in terms of broader Chinese

00:40:28.349 --> 00:40:34.609
policies now in the East China Sea, the South
China Sea. Over the last three decades, China's

00:40:34.609 --> 00:40:41.760
been remarkably successful, with the exception
of India, of finalizing and getting international

00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:45.780
recognition for its land borders, but it has
not on the maritime front, and I think that's

00:40:45.780 --> 00:40:51.200
where it's turning to right now, and it has
growing interest. It has growing capabilities.

00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:58.430
And I think it's useful for an audience like
this to think about American history and recall

00:40:58.430 --> 00:41:04.339
in 1823, when President Monroe declares the
Monroe Doctrine, and a Monroe Doctrine is

00:41:04.339 --> 00:41:10.320
declared at a point where America started
to define its interests as no European interference

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:11.359
in the Western Hemisphere.

00:41:11.359 --> 00:41:18.800
Now, if you go back to China, from the mid-1990s
until today, you look at their behavior in

00:41:18.800 --> 00:41:27.210
the maritime domain, and is there -- without
anybody in Beijing having a Xi Jinping doctrine

00:41:27.210 --> 00:41:31.930
locked up in the safe -- but just is there
an evolution of their thinking based upon

00:41:31.930 --> 00:41:38.790
capabilities and interests that they're reaching
a point where they -- they don't want U.S.

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:43.369
reconnaissance activities 12 nautical miles
off of their coast. They have a different

00:41:43.369 --> 00:41:50.690
view of the Senkaku and Diaoyu. They have
a different view of the Spratly Islands.

00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:55.530
So I think it's going to be very difficult
for us to manage these problems as they come

00:41:55.530 --> 00:42:01.140
up. You talked about the Philippines case.
Now we're talking about Senkaku-Diaoyu. But

00:42:01.140 --> 00:42:05.430
we also have to bear in mind, in terms of
the United States, our way of operating militarily

00:42:05.430 --> 00:42:10.420
in the Western Pacific since the end of the
Second World War was that was an American

00:42:10.420 --> 00:42:14.900
lake. And I think the Chinese are reaching
a point where they have a different interpretation

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:15.900
of this.

00:42:15.900 --> 00:42:20.080
That's not an argument for us to pull back
on any of our commitments, but we have to

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:25.780
be clear among ourselves in these kind of
discussions that that's -- that's the trajectory

00:42:25.780 --> 00:42:28.710
China is on, and that's going to be part of
the equation here.

00:42:28.710 --> 00:42:33.780
GREEN: There is a doctrine, by the way. It
was called the Near Sea Doctrine. It's evolved.

00:42:33.780 --> 00:42:39.451
I was actually briefed on it at the Academy
of Military Sciences a few years ago, Central

00:42:39.451 --> 00:42:43.390
Military Commission approved it. Not unlike
the Monroe Doctrine, the ends, ways and means

00:42:43.390 --> 00:42:49.030
don't match. It's more aspirational. But that's
the trend. I think it's pretty clear, yeah.

00:42:49.030 --> 00:42:54.069
HAASS: Right. Well, on that note, let's open
it up. Anything is fair game, either about

00:42:54.069 --> 00:42:58.190
the Chinese-Japanese relationship, or we can
broaden it out to other potential scenarios.

00:42:58.190 --> 00:43:00.670
Winston? Ambassador Lord, excuse me?

00:43:00.670 --> 00:43:06.609
QUESTION: Just call me Your Excellency, any
of those things will work.

00:43:06.609 --> 00:43:07.609
(LAUGHTER)

00:43:07.609 --> 00:43:12.480
Win Lord, International Rescue Committee.
It's good to see some old friends here. First,

00:43:12.480 --> 00:43:17.460
one quick comment. When Jeff was discussing
what we did in 2010, it reminded me of the

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:23.069
Taiwan missile crisis, where we -- clearly,
China was the provocative party there, but

00:43:23.069 --> 00:43:27.221
we moved two aircraft carriers, but at the
same time, we sent strong message to Taiwan

00:43:27.221 --> 00:43:31.819
not to treat it as a blank check and to China
that we weren't changing our one China policy.

00:43:31.819 --> 00:43:34.030
So there are echoes there.

00:43:34.030 --> 00:43:38.970
But my question to any of you is the following,
and you touched on it a little bit. What are

00:43:38.970 --> 00:43:43.410
the chances the following would work, that
the -- and maybe it's happening -- I seriously

00:43:43.410 --> 00:43:51.069
doubt it, given the atmospherics -- that China
and Japan have secret talks, Japan comes up

00:43:51.069 --> 00:43:57.069
with some formula of great ambiguity, a la
Shanghai communique, whatever it was, the

00:43:57.069 --> 00:44:02.400
'92 consensus with Taiwan, which basically
says -- I don't have the language, but our

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:06.820
Chinese friends think there's a dispute over
these islands. We don't think there's a dispute

00:44:06.820 --> 00:44:12.240
over these islands, so obviously we disagree.
China takes that as saying Japan agrees there's

00:44:12.240 --> 00:44:17.910
a dispute. Abe tells his people, I didn't
change our position, but it's enough, but

00:44:17.910 --> 00:44:23.770
that is only done by the Japanese in exchange
for prior Chinese assurance they would then

00:44:23.770 --> 00:44:30.390
de facto start cutting down on their patrols
and their probes and de facto making a more

00:44:30.390 --> 00:44:32.780
peaceful situation.

00:44:32.780 --> 00:44:35.670
Could this work? And is anybody trying it?

00:44:35.670 --> 00:44:43.400
BADER: I'll take the first shot. Winston,
it's completely logical. Therefore...

00:44:43.400 --> 00:44:45.460
HAASS: So it had no chance whatsoever?

00:44:45.460 --> 00:44:46.460
(LAUGHTER)

00:44:46.460 --> 00:44:52.510
BADER: I mean, I -- I had this discussion
with Japanese friends like a year ago, word

00:44:52.510 --> 00:44:59.550
for word. And the answer was -- I mean, there
were two problems, number one, that Abe wouldn't

00:44:59.550 --> 00:45:08.839
do it, no matter how artful the language was,
and, number two, what was the expectation

00:45:08.839 --> 00:45:16.400
of how far back the Chinese would go. In conversations
I had with Japanese visitors close to Abe,

00:45:16.400 --> 00:45:20.414
I -- you know, I told them, I thought the
best you're going to -- you're not going to

00:45:20.414 --> 00:45:26.980
get status quo ante. The Chinese are going
to continue to send a ship inside those territorial

00:45:26.980 --> 00:45:32.230
waters. Just they won't do it every week or
two. They might do it once per six months

00:45:32.230 --> 00:45:37.319
or once a year. They're going to feel the
need to continue to establish the principle

00:45:37.319 --> 00:45:39.210
that they have of presence there.

00:45:39.210 --> 00:45:43.500
And the answer I got from this gentleman close
to Abe was status quo ante is the only thing

00:45:43.500 --> 00:45:48.440
that is of the slightest interest to us. Now,
I have no idea if this was an official position,

00:45:48.440 --> 00:45:53.510
but those were the kind of feelers I got.
Now where we are now, in the wake of Yasukuni,

00:45:53.510 --> 00:45:58.119
is that almost any conversation is next to
impossible for, say, some months.

00:45:58.119 --> 00:46:03.730
GREEN: Yeah, I think in the near term, it's
impossible. But there are people in the Japanese

00:46:03.730 --> 00:46:08.290
foreign ministry, I think, who are thinking
along those lines and in the Chinese foreign

00:46:08.290 --> 00:46:16.970
ministry. The problem is that it will help
to lower the temperature, but the problem

00:46:16.970 --> 00:46:20.920
ultimately will be that China's move is very
reversible.

00:46:20.920 --> 00:46:27.220
And it reminds me of something that was proposed
to us when I was in the White House by the

00:46:27.220 --> 00:46:32.790
foreign ministry, if we stopped Taiwan arms
sales, China would pull back its missiles.

00:46:32.790 --> 00:46:36.619
Some people thought that was a great deal,
until they started doing the math and realizing

00:46:36.619 --> 00:46:41.579
our stopping Taiwan arms sales is much harder
to reverse than a quiet, you know, return

00:46:41.579 --> 00:46:43.619
of some mobile missiles in Fujian.

00:46:43.619 --> 00:46:48.339
So the foreign ministry in China tries these
things. They had an agreement with the Japanese

00:46:48.339 --> 00:46:52.880
on joint oil exploration, for example. But
as you know very well, Win, the foreign ministry

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:55.650
has a very hard time actually implementing
these.

00:46:55.650 --> 00:46:59.261
So for this to happen, it's going to have
to come out of Xi Jinping and the new national

00:46:59.261 --> 00:47:06.980
security council, or whatever they're translating
it as, and for the reason Jeff said, the temperature

00:47:06.980 --> 00:47:10.460
is just all wrong right now. But in the next
three years, I wouldn't entirely rule it out.

00:47:10.460 --> 00:47:13.630
It just doesn't solve the problem. All it
does is lower the temperature.

00:47:13.630 --> 00:47:18.410
EIKENBERRY: Yeah, the -- I think the key right
now is to find a way as rapidly as possible,

00:47:18.410 --> 00:47:25.010
if there is a way ahead, to demilitarize this
dispute, because it's extraordinarily dangerous.

00:47:25.010 --> 00:47:30.770
I don't know -- and, you know, my recent trips
to Beijing, I'm not certain at this juncture,

00:47:30.770 --> 00:47:36.490
though, Win, whether the Chinese leadership
is, as they've said, given up on Abe. Well,

00:47:36.490 --> 00:47:40.609
you can say you've given up on him and then
things can change next week and they'll talk

00:47:40.609 --> 00:47:41.609
to him again.

00:47:41.609 --> 00:47:46.530
But I do think we're at a point where it's
going to require the very highest levels of

00:47:46.530 --> 00:47:55.040
leadership, Abe and Xi, to set the tone, give
the signals for the two sides to sit down

00:47:55.040 --> 00:47:59.780
in a serious way behind closed doors, which
you're talking about, and I think there could

00:47:59.780 --> 00:48:06.430
be formulas there. I think that one of the
outcomes of that, though, would be a better

00:48:06.430 --> 00:48:13.109
outcome if there was also some kind of crises
management mechanism established to where

00:48:13.109 --> 00:48:18.290
the combined militaries and law enforcement
agencies were talking to each other on a daily

00:48:18.290 --> 00:48:24.430
basis, as I agree with you, Mike, that they'll
-- the Chinese will come back and they'll

00:48:24.430 --> 00:48:30.430
say, domestically, politically, they have
to keep on trying to find ways to say that

00:48:30.430 --> 00:48:37.010
they're asserting their administrative control
of Diaoyu, and with that, then, to have that

00:48:37.010 --> 00:48:42.970
crisis management mechanism established and
agreed transparency between the two sides

00:48:42.970 --> 00:48:50.150
I think would be awfully important to try
to sustain the agreement.

00:48:50.150 --> 00:48:52.290
HAASS: Yes, sir?

00:48:52.290 --> 00:48:58.390
QUESTION: Hi, Dan Rosen. Nice to see everybody.
There seems to be a consensus that the domestic

00:48:58.390 --> 00:49:03.390
utility of this conflict is one of the major
drivers here, that this is for domestic consumption

00:49:03.390 --> 00:49:08.870
on both sides. On the Chinese side, what's
going on right now that's so different than

00:49:08.870 --> 00:49:14.960
a year ago that justifies, in terms of domestic
utility, this level of tension? Because there's

00:49:14.960 --> 00:49:20.190
some Chinese optionality and choice in how
high to turn the dial here, I would guess.

00:49:20.190 --> 00:49:23.450
It's not just a reaction to Japan. It's a
choice on Beijing's side.

00:49:23.450 --> 00:49:27.480
So what is it in the political economy in
Beijing right now that makes this reasonable

00:49:27.480 --> 00:49:29.720
from a domestic, political perspective?

00:49:29.720 --> 00:49:37.599
BADER: Well, let me take an opening shot,
Dan. I would not answer the question directly.

00:49:37.599 --> 00:49:44.020
I mean, what I would say is that I think the
reason why China's tone has changed so dramatically

00:49:44.020 --> 00:49:50.790
in the last two years is because of Abe and
because of the perception of the security

00:49:50.790 --> 00:50:01.369
policies that Abe is pursuing. And the desire
to depict Abe as a throwback to the 1930s

00:50:01.369 --> 00:50:09.730
to try to rally Northeast and Southeast Asian
regional opinion against him as a way of sort

00:50:09.730 --> 00:50:18.119
of putting some spokes -- some brakes on his
intended direction on security policy. I think

00:50:18.119 --> 00:50:26.359
that's fundamentally what is underlying the
volume coming out of Beijing now.

00:50:26.359 --> 00:50:31.180
HAASS: You don't think it has to do, also,
with the slowdown of economic growth, a new

00:50:31.180 --> 00:50:36.890
leadership trying to establish itself, cutting
a closer relationship with the military, trying

00:50:36.890 --> 00:50:41.410
to harness nationalism for its own domestic
political purposes?

00:50:41.410 --> 00:50:48.809
EIKENBERRY: My own view would be, yes, that's
a factor, and it certainly is. And I think,

00:50:48.809 --> 00:50:54.920
though, the Chinese leaders collectively,
you know, led by Xi right now are also aware

00:50:54.920 --> 00:50:59.940
that they're facing an extraordinary array
right now of domestic problems. They're trying

00:50:59.940 --> 00:51:05.819
to restructure their economy, which has got
huge political consequences. They're facing

00:51:05.819 --> 00:51:11.230
an environment which is so polluted that they're
worried about grassroots movements starting

00:51:11.230 --> 00:51:17.580
to form and network within China against the
party's leadership. We all know what this

00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:18.580
array of problems is.

00:51:18.580 --> 00:51:22.660
And what that leads to, then, is the conclusion
of the third plenum of the 18th Party Congress

00:51:22.660 --> 00:51:28.250
that what they need for the next decade is
they need stability. They need stability in

00:51:28.250 --> 00:51:33.109
China, and they can only maintain stability
in China if they have a region that's stable

00:51:33.109 --> 00:51:37.290
and global security situation which is generally
favorable.

00:51:37.290 --> 00:51:43.730
So they're not in a real maverick mood right
now. They also are very aware of second- and

00:51:43.730 --> 00:51:50.790
third-order consequences. When they try to
play the nationalist card, if they do, they

00:51:50.790 --> 00:51:56.660
have a history of playing that card, and then
the Japanese -- the anti-Japanese protests

00:51:56.660 --> 00:52:00.829
suddenly take on an anti-party tone to them.

00:52:00.829 --> 00:52:06.400
But all of that said, I would agree, Richard,
that Xi Jinping taking over -- and he's very

00:52:06.400 --> 00:52:13.570
activist. In every area, you hear -- we used
to hear about Hu and Wen, now you only hear

00:52:13.570 --> 00:52:20.030
about Xi. And that's in domestic politics.
That's in terms of security policies and foreign

00:52:20.030 --> 00:52:24.119
policies, and perhaps part of it trying to
be a strong commander-in-chief and get the

00:52:24.119 --> 00:52:26.559
People's Liberation Army lined up behind him.

00:52:26.559 --> 00:52:30.131
HAASS: Just out of curiosity, where does the
establishment or the declaration of the new

00:52:30.131 --> 00:52:35.110
air defense zone, what's your interpretation
of that? Is that an exception to this? Is

00:52:35.110 --> 00:52:38.810
this part of it? I mean, did the Chinese miscalculate?
What's your...

00:52:38.810 --> 00:52:47.410
GREEN: I think this is part of a playbook
that was established under this maritime doctrine,

00:52:47.410 --> 00:52:51.940
with ADIZs somewhere in our future in the
West Sea or...

00:52:51.940 --> 00:52:56.140
HAASS: Please explain -- don't -- you can't
use that -- you can't say ADIZs. No one knows

00:52:56.140 --> 00:52:58.140
what you're talking about.

00:52:58.140 --> 00:53:03.599
GREEN: Sorry, air defense identification zone,
which is a mouthful. Can I say ADIZ now? Because

00:53:03.599 --> 00:53:09.559
there's going to be another one in Yellow
or West Sea, west coast of Korea, and in the

00:53:09.559 --> 00:53:12.630
South China Sea. And this is salami slicing.

00:53:12.630 --> 00:53:20.040
I'm told -- and Jeff or Karl may know better
-- that the leadership actually expected Vice

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:24.309
President Biden to cancel his trip over this
and that the U.S. response – though still

00:53:24.309 --> 00:53:27.790
quite firm on the military side, and I think
if they felt that they misread our politics,

00:53:27.790 --> 00:53:32.660
our foreign policy, but that the U.S. response
was perhaps less than they expected.

00:53:32.660 --> 00:53:37.020
So I think this is -- this is not a one-off.
I think step by step, we're going to see more

00:53:37.020 --> 00:53:41.059
and more of these things. And we'll just have
to take them as they come, but we have to

00:53:41.059 --> 00:53:47.900
put it in part of a larger strategic construct
we don't quite have yet.

00:53:47.900 --> 00:53:49.280
HAASS: Roger?

00:53:49.280 --> 00:53:54.599
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE) Capital. The tone, of
course, in Washington, when I got there four

00:53:54.599 --> 00:54:02.270
years ago, was that they got a lot of economics
at stake and they won't do anything to put

00:54:02.270 --> 00:54:06.400
that at risk. So I think that National Security
Council meeting that you're convening here

00:54:06.400 --> 00:54:13.190
today, Richard, would have a lot on the economic
aspects of it, particularly what kind of economic

00:54:13.190 --> 00:54:17.130
carrots and sticks can be brought to the table.

00:54:17.130 --> 00:54:24.210
And you have to realize the Chinese have numerous
grievances against us, and then generally,

00:54:24.210 --> 00:54:33.650
on many things, but it -- the bullying started
with the rare earth embargo in 2010. And the

00:54:33.650 --> 00:54:40.380
first grievances that we actually went forward
a WTO case against China, on the case of rare

00:54:40.380 --> 00:54:45.260
earth, of market manipulation, and that's
still ongoing.

00:54:45.260 --> 00:54:52.059
But they have grievances on investment. They're
very active in going to the Financial Times

00:54:52.059 --> 00:54:57.140
and other newspapers complaining that they're
not treated fairly by the Committee on Foreign

00:54:57.140 --> 00:55:03.950
Investment in the United States, CFIUS. They
have grievances that they -- that we goaded

00:55:03.950 --> 00:55:11.881
them for support -- financial support for
countries in trouble at the IMF and the World

00:55:11.881 --> 00:55:17.390
Bank, but they don't get the quid pro quo
of American support for them having greater

00:55:17.390 --> 00:55:24.410
influence on decision-making. And they think
that TPP, the Trans-Pacific Partnership negotiations,

00:55:24.410 --> 00:55:31.280
is -- one, the economic side of the pivot,
namely, a containment policy against China,

00:55:31.280 --> 00:55:33.369
and they say they don't want to be contained.

00:55:33.369 --> 00:55:41.049
So each one of those grievances gives an opportunity
and a risk. The opportunity is, meet some

00:55:41.049 --> 00:55:47.960
of their objections. Open things up. Sell
them more military high-tech products, which

00:55:47.960 --> 00:55:53.410
is in every speech that all of the Chinese
leaders always say. You think that trade is

00:55:53.410 --> 00:55:59.670
about foreign exchange rates; we think it's
because of the stuff that you won't sell us.

00:55:59.670 --> 00:56:05.190
On the other hand, we need the Chinese on
things like Iranian sanctions enforcement.

00:56:05.190 --> 00:56:13.230
And so we are in a much more complex economic
and financial situation with China than any

00:56:13.230 --> 00:56:18.610
adversary you can dredge up from the Cold
War. We didn't have any of these kinds of

00:56:18.610 --> 00:56:20.531
links with the Russians.

00:56:20.531 --> 00:56:23.390
HAASS: Let me just mention one thing which
Roger didn't, which is our dependence in part

00:56:23.390 --> 00:56:30.220
on Chinese dollar holdings and our need for
them to participate in the -- our lifestyle,

00:56:30.220 --> 00:56:36.589
essentially, sustaining our deficit and our
debt. How much does that constrain us, if

00:56:36.589 --> 00:56:42.049
we were -- if we wanted to suddenly side with
Japan and we suddenly saw the set of the -- the

00:56:42.049 --> 00:56:46.359
head of the Chinese central bank suddenly
announce that they were going to cease dollar

00:56:46.359 --> 00:56:52.060
purchases until such a time as the United
States backed off and reconsidered its deployment

00:56:52.060 --> 00:56:54.960
of forces, where would that put us?

00:56:54.960 --> 00:56:55.960
GREEN: They can hurt us. They can hurt us.

00:56:55.960 --> 00:57:00.059
HAASS: I meant Treasury purchases, but you
know.

00:57:00.059 --> 00:57:06.830
GREEN: They can hurt us, but if you continue
down that escalation or up that escalation

00:57:06.830 --> 00:57:14.260
logic, a global economic meltdown is bad for
us. It's terminal for the Chinese Communist

00:57:14.260 --> 00:57:18.799
Party. And obviously, we want to avoid that,
but we shouldn't be more afraid of this than

00:57:18.799 --> 00:57:19.799
necessary.

00:57:19.799 --> 00:57:26.559
And Abe spends 80 percent of his time on the
economy. And I imagine that for Xi Jinping

00:57:26.559 --> 00:57:29.680
it's a comparable amount of his time. Abe's
told people this. I imagine for Xi Jinping

00:57:29.680 --> 00:57:30.680
it's comparable.

00:57:30.680 --> 00:57:32.970
So it's not like either leader -- well, I
think Jeff put it well. They have -- there's

00:57:32.970 --> 00:57:38.180
a utility in a certain amount of friction.
Abe would not have come back politically without

00:57:38.180 --> 00:57:45.200
China's pressure on Japan. But these economic
factors, I think, are very heavy in both leaders'

00:57:45.200 --> 00:57:46.200
minds.

00:57:46.200 --> 00:57:52.950
And we, for our part (OFF-MIKE) we're not
checkmated. I don't think this fundamentally

00:57:52.950 --> 00:57:56.850
changes our toolkit in how we would deal with
a crisis like this.

00:57:56.850 --> 00:58:02.240
EIKENBERRY: I guess I'd make two points here,
the first on the degree of interdependency,

00:58:02.240 --> 00:58:06.960
not only between the United States and China,
but between China and Japan and China -- and

00:58:06.960 --> 00:58:12.700
many other countries. It's pretty striking.
Last year's bilateral trade, over $500 billion

00:58:12.700 --> 00:58:19.181
between the United States and China, the Chinese
hold $1.2 trillion of Treasury notes. They're

00:58:19.181 --> 00:58:27.260
our largest source of imports. They're – China
is our third-largest source of -- or destination

00:58:27.260 --> 00:58:33.380
for our exports. In the case of Japan, their
largest bilateral trading partner is China.

00:58:33.380 --> 00:58:37.359
So I've heard people now talk about, do you
remember during the Cold War days that we

00:58:37.359 --> 00:58:42.109
had mutually assured destruction with nuclear
weapons? Now people talk about mutually assured

00:58:42.109 --> 00:58:47.220
economic destruction. And that's the world
that we live in.

00:58:47.220 --> 00:58:53.400
I would -- I would take a little bit of note,
though, on the points you had made about our

00:58:53.400 --> 00:58:59.250
economic relations with China. Actually, there
are -- maybe two years ago, I would have agreed.

00:58:59.250 --> 00:59:07.319
I don't think that I would, though. The currency
problems that were really first on the agenda,

00:59:07.319 --> 00:59:14.780
those seem to be managed now in a pretty good
way. The Chinese are very interested in a

00:59:14.780 --> 00:59:18.630
bilateral investment treaty with the United
States, and now they've come forward in agreement,

00:59:18.630 --> 00:59:23.780
they'll have a negative list, rather than
the positive, which we couldn't have moved

00:59:23.780 --> 00:59:24.780
forward with.

00:59:24.780 --> 00:59:28.430
And interestingly, on TPP (inaudible) agree
with you. Two years ago, you heard from the

00:59:28.430 --> 00:59:35.040
Chinese, TPP is part of containment. Now,
increasingly, you're hearing the Chinese economists,

00:59:35.040 --> 00:59:39.280
political leaders say if we can get a BIT,
then maybe TPP is next, because it could very

00:59:39.280 --> 00:59:43.049
well be in China's interest, because if you
look at their reform package that they've

00:59:43.049 --> 00:59:49.680
announced with the third of the 18th, then
the TPP could also be a very good model for

00:59:49.680 --> 00:59:52.200
them to follow for the enactment of reforms.

00:59:52.200 --> 00:59:57.340
HAASS: I apologize for the use of acronyms.
That's -- BIT is a bilateral investment treaty.

00:59:57.340 --> 01:00:02.920
TPP is the trade agreement that's being negotiated
across the Pacific. And I will try to get

01:00:02.920 --> 01:00:05.030
these people here to desist from the...

01:00:05.030 --> 01:00:06.069
(UNKNOWN): We're from Washington.

01:00:06.069 --> 01:00:10.940
HAASS: ... ADIZs and other such things, but
clearly, I'm failing in my -- in my efforts.

01:00:10.940 --> 01:00:11.940
(CROSSTALK)

01:00:11.940 --> 01:00:14.720
HAASS: There was somebody here. Yes, sir,
John?

01:00:14.720 --> 01:00:20.150
QUESTION: Thanks. John Makin, Cornwall Capital
and American Enterprise Institute. Listening

01:00:20.150 --> 01:00:26.010
to you gentlemen makes me wonder if the island
dispute isn't a red herring. That is, given

01:00:26.010 --> 01:00:31.040
the range of issues you've discussed, let's
say we could -- we solve the problem tomorrow

01:00:31.040 --> 01:00:36.900
and both China and Japan say we don't care
about this pile of rocks -- not likely, but

01:00:36.900 --> 01:00:42.829
it's a good thought experiment -- in my view,
we'd still have some serious problems. And

01:00:42.829 --> 01:00:48.849
as I think it's been pointed out, the Chinese
leadership probably hasn't planned on how

01:00:48.849 --> 01:00:57.250
aggressive Mr. Abe is being, and yet Mr. Abe
is where he is because he's being aggressive.

01:00:57.250 --> 01:01:03.990
What role -- and you've touched on some of
this, but is there a role that the United

01:01:03.990 --> 01:01:10.950
States can play or can hope to play, or should
we just assume that we'll -- that there will

01:01:10.950 --> 01:01:15.980
be a conflict of some sort and do a lot more
contingency planning?

01:01:15.980 --> 01:01:23.180
GREEN: Well, we should do contingency planning,
because there are probably 20,000 people in

01:01:23.180 --> 01:01:25.790
Washington who depend on that for their livelihood.
No, because...

01:01:25.790 --> 01:01:26.790
(LAUGHTER)

01:01:26.790 --> 01:01:27.790
Because...

01:01:27.790 --> 01:01:28.790
HAASS: Is this a jobs program?

01:01:28.790 --> 01:01:33.930
GREEN: As I said earlier, we need to be thinking
ahead about this jointly with the Japanese

01:01:33.930 --> 01:01:42.220
so that we can urge restraint and do it from
a position where they'll be confident. The

01:01:42.220 --> 01:01:51.170
-- the Chinese made Abe. He was in the wilderness.
He was finished politically, most people think.

01:01:51.170 --> 01:01:55.049
He came back because the Chinese were beating
up the government in power. Without China,

01:01:55.049 --> 01:01:58.360
there would have been no recovery for Mr.
Abe.

01:01:58.360 --> 01:02:04.089
But I would say it's important to briefly,
very briefly define what he's doing. And people

01:02:04.089 --> 01:02:10.329
talk about it as being aggressive. And the
shrine visit certainly didn't help with management

01:02:10.329 --> 01:02:11.339
of that issue for him.

01:02:11.339 --> 01:02:16.260
But if you set that aside for a moment and
just look at his actual policies, he's increasing

01:02:16.260 --> 01:02:22.280
the defense budget by about 0.8 percent this
year after 11 years of decline. The Chinese

01:02:22.280 --> 01:02:27.270
defense budget has been double-digit for almost
two decades. He said -- not that he's going

01:02:27.270 --> 01:02:31.849
to revise the constitution -- that will take
time, he knows it -- he's going to change

01:02:31.849 --> 01:02:35.730
some of the interpretations to allow a more
collective defense with the U.S., so that

01:02:35.730 --> 01:02:40.830
we can have the kind of virtual joint and
combined relationships and plan together and

01:02:40.830 --> 01:02:45.010
do more together that we do with Korea or
Australia or Canada or any number of allies.

01:02:45.010 --> 01:02:50.039
He's actually slowed down that process, to
the frustration of many in the Pentagon.

01:02:50.039 --> 01:02:55.869
So it's important to keep it in perspective.
He's not, you know, looking out for demons

01:02:55.869 --> 01:02:59.590
to slay. He's spending, as I said, 80 percent
of his economy on what keeps him in power

01:02:59.590 --> 01:03:03.470
in a parliamentary system, which is growing
the economy, and in that, not so unlike Xi

01:03:03.470 --> 01:03:04.470
Jinping.

01:03:04.470 --> 01:03:06.640
HAASS: Yes, ma'am?

01:03:06.640 --> 01:03:13.430
QUESTION: My name is Contessa Bourbon from
the New York Times. How possible it is that

01:03:13.430 --> 01:03:19.520
the United Nations can settle the territorial
dispute? Has the Philippines filed a case

01:03:19.520 --> 01:03:26.369
against China at the U.N.? And is it possible
that conflict between Japan and China can

01:03:26.369 --> 01:03:29.039
also be settled through the United Nations?

01:03:29.039 --> 01:03:31.840
HAASS: (OFF-MIKE) about the Philippine case
(OFF-MIKE)

01:03:31.840 --> 01:03:32.840
EIKENBERRY: Whether, you know, broadly...

01:03:32.840 --> 01:03:33.840
HAASS: Whether that's a precedent in any way.

01:03:33.840 --> 01:03:36.490
EIKENBERRY: ... whether these territorial
disputes can be solved by the United Nations

01:03:36.490 --> 01:03:37.490
arbitration.

01:03:37.490 --> 01:03:40.500
BADER: Look, you know, I think that the Philippines
-- you know, the Philippines did not consult

01:03:40.500 --> 01:03:48.589
us before they went to the U.N. Law of the
Sea tribunal. And I think it's a good thing

01:03:48.589 --> 01:03:51.910
they didn't consult us. I think the U.S. would
have discouraged them from going there, and

01:03:51.910 --> 01:03:55.869
I think it's a very good thing that they went
there. I think it was very smart.

01:03:55.869 --> 01:04:02.299
They have good lawyers, and they've come up
with a very good set of arguments, which focus

01:04:02.299 --> 01:04:06.400
on the issue of the nine-dash line in the
South China Sea. And for those of you who

01:04:06.400 --> 01:04:11.270
are not familiar with it, basically, that's
a line that the Chinese inherited for the

01:04:11.270 --> 01:04:17.010
Kuomintang government, which covers 3.5 million
square kilometers in the South Pacific and

01:04:17.010 --> 01:04:23.470
in which the Chinese assert some sort of unspecified
claims perhaps to all of the waters. It's

01:04:23.470 --> 01:04:24.470
not clear.

01:04:24.470 --> 01:04:29.180
Anyway, the Philippine case is challenging
that. My guess is the Philippines will not

01:04:29.180 --> 01:04:33.641
do too badly in the outcome of the case. It
won't be an unambiguous outcome, but they

01:04:33.641 --> 01:04:37.280
may do fairly well in it. And the Chinese
will ignore the outcome.

01:04:37.280 --> 01:04:42.210
But I think it will help spur a debate within
China. There are a lot of lawyers in China,

01:04:42.210 --> 01:04:48.680
there a lot of people who are looking at these
kinds of issues, and I think it will help

01:04:48.680 --> 01:04:54.460
the people who within China take international
law seriously, who take globalization seriously,

01:04:54.460 --> 01:05:00.910
and it will provide an argument, not for immediate,
you know, surrender to the Philippine position.

01:05:00.910 --> 01:05:06.109
Now, this doesn't really go to the sovereignty
issues. I cannot picture a situation where

01:05:06.109 --> 01:05:13.690
the parties go to U.N. tribunals on sovereignty
issues. Look, I mean, the Japanese have offered

01:05:13.690 --> 01:05:21.549
to go to the world court on the Takeshima-Dokdo
issue, and the South Koreans have refused.

01:05:21.549 --> 01:05:26.010
South Koreans would almost certainly win in
the case where they go, but they refuse, even

01:05:26.010 --> 01:05:32.380
though they'd win. I mean, there's -- the
nationalism trumps the logic and the immediate

01:05:32.380 --> 01:05:34.240
advantage in most of these cases.

01:05:34.240 --> 01:05:39.530
EIKENBERRY: And possession, also -- also has
something to do it, as the Koreans have got

01:05:39.530 --> 01:05:40.530
possession.

01:05:40.530 --> 01:05:41.530
BADER: Yeah, that's right.

01:05:41.530 --> 01:05:43.049
EIKENBERRY: So they don't want to take it
into arbitration.

01:05:43.049 --> 01:05:47.799
GREEN: If there's -- if there's a diplomatic
process that could help, aside from confidence-building,

01:05:47.799 --> 01:05:53.859
which Karl and I have discussed, between the
militaries, it's all about fish. And Japan

01:05:53.859 --> 01:05:58.950
and Korea were at odds over Dokdo or Takeshima,
but in '98, they signed a fisheries agreement,

01:05:58.950 --> 01:06:02.831
and the issue subsided until the fisheries
agreement started falling apart. And it was

01:06:02.831 --> 01:06:06.960
local fishermen in Japan that started raising
this issue.

01:06:06.960 --> 01:06:11.380
Taiwan just reached an agreement with Japan.
Taiwan also claims the Diaoyutai-Senkakus.

01:06:11.380 --> 01:06:17.210
That fisheries agreement has calmed down a
lot of Taiwanese fishermen who were trying

01:06:17.210 --> 01:06:21.440
to agitate and cause trouble. So if there's
an area for diplomacy that would make a difference,

01:06:21.440 --> 01:06:24.849
in addition to military-to-military confidence-building,
it might be fish.

01:06:24.849 --> 01:06:28.930
The stocks are moving north because of climate
change, so it's the fishes' fault. If we can

01:06:28.930 --> 01:06:34.390
find a way to harvest them together more peacefully,
that -- that actually in the past has gone

01:06:34.390 --> 01:06:39.420
-- in the recent past, with Japan and Taiwan,
a long way towards lowering the temperature

01:06:39.420 --> 01:06:40.420
on these issues.

01:06:40.420 --> 01:06:43.309
HAASS: I've been to a lot of Council meetings.
I've never heard anybody blame a dispute on

01:06:43.309 --> 01:06:47.900
the fish before, and just -- at some point,
someone will speak up for the fish.

01:06:47.900 --> 01:06:51.480
Jeff, you get the last question.

01:06:51.480 --> 01:06:59.109
QUESTION: Jeff Laurenti. I wonder if you might
tell us, what is the view in Tokyo, in Beijing,

01:06:59.109 --> 01:07:05.450
if any, of the American now phase-out, perhaps
fade to black in Afghanistan? That is, is

01:07:05.450 --> 01:07:11.220
this seen as a sign of either lost American
credibility? Is it a sign simply that the

01:07:11.220 --> 01:07:15.180
Americans have done their best and this is
a hopeless situation, a little bit of sympathy?

01:07:15.180 --> 01:07:20.730
And particularly for the Chinese, who actually
have a border, small, but a border with Afghanistan,

01:07:20.730 --> 01:07:27.109
and that have in the Uighur parts of Xinjiang,
major problems with jihadism, what is the

01:07:27.109 --> 01:07:32.720
stake for the Chinese in a post kind of American
presence in Afghanistan in trying to keep

01:07:32.720 --> 01:07:37.359
the Taliban out? What is their likely role?
One assumes the Japanese have no interest

01:07:37.359 --> 01:07:38.520
or stake at all there.

01:07:38.520 --> 01:07:42.150
EIKENBERRY: Well, maybe I can talk from Beijing's
view.

01:07:42.150 --> 01:07:44.280
(UNKNOWN): Know something about Afghanistan?

01:07:44.280 --> 01:07:51.660
EIKENBERRY: The -- I think what the Chinese
worry -- the only thing they worry about more

01:07:51.660 --> 01:07:57.440
than Americans having a long-term presence
in Afghanistan is the Americans not having

01:07:57.440 --> 01:08:03.450
a long-term presence in Afghanistan, because
as the uncertainty now is out there for all

01:08:03.450 --> 01:08:09.240
of us to be concerned about, about what's
going to happen to our military relationship

01:08:09.240 --> 01:08:14.050
and our security agreements with Afghanistan.
After 2014, will we even have one? The Chinese

01:08:14.050 --> 01:08:16.230
are increasingly concerned.

01:08:16.230 --> 01:08:20.921
And why would they be concerned? You already
mentioned one, the possibility, then, of Al

01:08:20.921 --> 01:08:26.210
Qaida and their confederates. Specifically,
they worry about the Uzbekistan independent

01:08:26.210 --> 01:08:34.600
movement gaining sanctuary, again, inside
of Afghanistan. They're worried, also, I think,

01:08:34.600 --> 01:08:39.679
geopolitically about if they see the return
of a great -- new great game actors, but the

01:08:39.679 --> 01:08:44.480
return of the great game, that they should
-- they would have great problems on their

01:08:44.480 --> 01:08:50.520
western flank, and that could extend into
Central Asia, where they've made extraordinary

01:08:50.520 --> 01:08:57.760
investments in terms of resource development,
natural gas, and oil, pipeline development,

01:08:57.760 --> 01:09:03.140
and they also worry about the potential impact
on instability in Pakistan.

01:09:03.140 --> 01:09:08.980
So we are talking to the Chinese about Afghanistan.
We talk about bilateral cooperation programs,

01:09:08.980 --> 01:09:14.029
where I think the bigger opportunity is -- and
the imperative is, I think the Chinese understand

01:09:14.029 --> 01:09:18.339
this, is that even if the United States of
America and Beijing were to sit down and say

01:09:18.339 --> 01:09:23.580
that the two of us together and alone were
going to solve this problem, it's beyond our

01:09:23.580 --> 01:09:29.699
reach, because there's a lot of other actors
who get votes, Pakistan, India, Iran, Russia,

01:09:29.699 --> 01:09:33.980
and -- and the European Union, NATO can help.

01:09:33.980 --> 01:09:38.460
Each one of those actors that are out there,
we probably -- we, the Chinese and the Americans,

01:09:38.460 --> 01:09:44.290
have got comparative advantages in influence
and leverage that we have, so can we sit behind

01:09:44.290 --> 01:09:49.449
closed doors and come up with a collaborative
strategy, but knowing that it's not going

01:09:49.449 --> 01:09:56.179
to be bilaterally implemented, and see if,
as Xi Jinping and our -- and President Obama

01:09:56.179 --> 01:10:01.650
have talked about, a new kind of relationship
going forward? It seems to me that's the kind

01:10:01.650 --> 01:10:06.040
of relationship we should be working for,
that we don't want to minimize problems over

01:10:06.040 --> 01:10:11.449
Senkaku and Diaoyu. We certainly want to work
to avoid a crisis catching us by surprise.

01:10:11.449 --> 01:10:16.739
But there might be some greater global opportunities
for collaboration and alignment. I think Afghanistan's

01:10:16.739 --> 01:10:18.050
one of them.

01:10:18.050 --> 01:10:19.050
HAASS: And on that...

01:10:19.050 --> 01:10:20.050
GREEN: Just on -- oh, sorry.

01:10:20.050 --> 01:10:21.050
HAASS: Go ahead. No, go ahead.

01:10:21.050 --> 01:10:24.690
GREEN: Real quick on Japan. The Japanese at
our request invested very heavily in stability

01:10:24.690 --> 01:10:29.900
and development in Afghanistan, second largest
after us, in terms of ODA. And so I'm sure

01:10:29.900 --> 01:10:32.170
they're frustrated and disappointed, as we
are.

01:10:32.170 --> 01:10:37.480
But the event in that part of the world that
has struck at the Japanese and Korean confidence

01:10:37.480 --> 01:10:41.870
in the U.S. was not Afghanistan. It was Syria.
And it wasn't that they wanted us to use force,

01:10:41.870 --> 01:10:46.120
necessarily, but the fact that we -- that
the president said we would use force and

01:10:46.120 --> 01:10:49.900
then threw it to the Congress, to the Koreans
looking at North Korea with chemical weapons,

01:10:49.900 --> 01:10:54.540
by the way, and the Japanese looking at China,
that was unnerving. And that is what you hear

01:10:54.540 --> 01:10:57.700
about when you talk to senior people in those
capitals.

01:10:57.700 --> 01:10:58.700
BADER: Just...

01:10:58.700 --> 01:11:01.150
HAASS: Sure. Yeah, the last one.

01:11:01.150 --> 01:11:08.320
BADER: The U.S. dialogue with China on Pakistan,
which I have observed for years, is incredibly

01:11:08.320 --> 01:11:15.320
sterile. The Chinese do not wish to have a
serious dialogue on Pakistan. They wish to

01:11:15.320 --> 01:11:19.530
own the Pakistan relationship, and they don't
want us to have any visibility on it.

01:11:19.530 --> 01:11:25.010
I think that Afghanistan is a bit of an offshoot
of that in a sense. I think Karl is right,

01:11:25.010 --> 01:11:31.010
that we do in theory have more in common there,
and we should be able to do more, but I fear

01:11:31.010 --> 01:11:36.290
that the Chinese approach to Afghanistan is
somewhat reflective of their approach on Pakistan,

01:11:36.290 --> 01:11:40.030
which, of course, they use as a hedge against
India.

01:11:40.030 --> 01:11:44.720
HAASS: I don't know how to say be careful
what you wish for in Mandarin, but anyone

01:11:44.720 --> 01:11:50.260
who wants to own the Pakistan relationship,
I say, be careful what you wish for.

01:11:50.260 --> 01:11:51.260
(LAUGHTER)

01:11:51.260 --> 01:11:56.190
This is interesting. A little bit more sanguine
than I thought, and I hope you're right. I'm

01:11:56.190 --> 01:12:00.590
not persuaded you are. But, again, I want
to thank you actually for two things. I want

01:12:00.590 --> 01:12:07.270
to thank you for tonight, but amongst these
three gentlemen, we have literally decades

01:12:07.270 --> 01:12:11.230
of service to our government and our country,
so I want to thank them for that, as well.

01:12:11.230 --> 01:12:11.540
(APPLAUSE)

